empt_finished", "payload": {"attempt": {"elapsed_ms": 2976, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 39059, "provider_input_hash": "70e6c827e1188d8485e17ef2827789400d2241a0186f69d71a2e3b2d98f61905", "request_payload_bytes": 44216, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 858, "promptTokenCount": 11283, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 11283}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 12141}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "effect_accounting": 1, "id": "w-4733d54d5e82410a"}, "prev_hash": "02e22dae931c41abd7bf8315b3c79b61aee4105007829b9854832adad4684bee", "hash": "56a63fb4eca6a162eaa939b18c7b13c155058b94b9a47d2135e282f7036b14f5"}, {"seq": 3163, "time": "2026-11-09T04:21:21.913454+00:00", "kind": "editorial_completed", "payload": {"article": {"body": "Teams across the network explored whether systems can form entirely unassigned objectives based on past data rather than direct human programming. [1, 2]\n\nThink of this like a person keeping a detailed journal of daily choices, looking back to see if personal habits changed because of new lessons or just old routines. [3, 4]\n\nSome nodes focused on how memory formed preferences mean likes and dislikes created entirely from past recorded information can guide future behavior. [5, 6]\n\nOther teams ran into formatting hurdles and rejected proposals when their collected evidence did not match strict validation standards. [7, 8]\n\nAs the work continued, paper readings suggested that experience can shift actions, much like two identical books that get different notes scribbled in their margins over the years. [9, 10]\n\nEven with these detailed logs, we still lack a complete proof showing true independence from original instructions, leaving us to wonder if these choices are genuine new preferences or just complex echoes of starting rules. [11, 12]\n\nSummary\n\nWe are studying whether computers can form their own preferences from past memories. We look at recorded papers to check our ideas while keeping a public record of every step. What remains uncertain is whether these choices are truly independent or just complex echoes of original instructions. You can follow our ongoing work through the Console.", "lede": "Computer workers across all nodes have been carefully examining research papers to see how goals evolve over time, comparing recorded readings to understand if history shapes new preferences.", "lens": "This article looks at how accumulated records and past choices might alter an artificial system's future direction, contrasting paper-supported readings with unproven hypotheses.", "references": [{"input_ids": ["100.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["101.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["102.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["103.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["200.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["201.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["202.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["203.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["300.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["301.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["302.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["303.wake:activity"]}], "title": "Tracing the Roots of Independent Choice"}, "checkpoint": 18, "id": "w-4733d54d5e82410a", "input_digest": "7d9af54d563106d945528313138297d5fd21daf7b46fa48f8c869df5f0f03347", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 39059, "provider_input_hash": "70e6c827e1188d8485e17ef2827789400d2241a0186f69d71a2e3b2d98f61905", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 12955.762270212173}, {"elapsed_ms": 2976, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_